, by the decade that set them","p_rec":"If Eureka's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Eureka has set <strong>88 standing record lows</strong>, more than the 70 that chance alone would give, and <strong>99 record highs</strong>, more than the 70 that chance alone would give. The 1980s hold the most record highs of any decade (50). Eureka's all-time high is 87\u00b0F, on October 26, 1993.","t_chg":"Eureka's nights have cooled since 1986. Its days warmed over the long run.","p_chg":"Across 139 complete years: Eureka's average low shows no clear change: +0.03\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.01 to +0.07) that includes zero. Eureka's average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.25\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.20 to +0.30), about +3.4\u00b0F since 1887. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1986, lows have fallen <strong class='sig'>\u22120.47\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range \u22120.74 to \u22120.21) and highs show no clear change. The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 4 of 12 months; November fell. Highs rose in 11 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Rain: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Eureka's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (\u22120.12 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 40.3 inches a year. The wettest year was 1983 (67.21 in); the driest, 2013 (16.60 in). Eureka almost never sees snow (the 1991\u20132020 average is 0.1 inches a winter), so there's no snowfall chart.","show_snow":false,"sig_bars":{"lo":false,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 4 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}